        payload: type-ref #141 10296:177:54-10302:177:60 name="String"
    declaration: enum-decl #150 10626:183:1-10713:188:1 name="@2::Answer" type-parameters=[] doc="What an `App` returns for a request. `Whole` is a whole response, as a `Handler` returns. `Stream` is a response\nwhose body arrives on a channel. `Upgrade` is what `accept_websocket` makes: its\n`Accept` runs a WebSocket on the connection, and its `Reply` (a request that is no handshake) is sent as `Whole`."
      variant: enum-variant #145 10640:184:3-10665:185:1 name="Whole"
        payload: type-ref #144 10646:184:9-10663:184:26 name="HttpWire.Response"
      variant: enum-variant #147 10667:185:3-10690:186:1 name="Stream"
        payload: type-ref #146 10674:185:10-10688:185:24 name="@2::StreamResponse"
      variant: enum-variant #149 10692:186:3-10709:187:1 name="Upgrade"
        payload: type-ref #148 10700:186:11-10707:186:18 name="@2::Upgrade"
    declaration: type-alias-decl #156 11044:192:1-11105:193:1 name="@2::App" doc="The function that `serve` and `serve_tls` call for each request: the request with an empty `body`, the\n`RequestBody` that carries the body as it arrives, and the `Answer` out. A route that wants the body whole calls\n`read_all`; `app` makes an `App` from a `Handler`. A middleware is a function from an `App` to an `App`."
      target: type-ref #155 11055:192:12-11104:192:61 name="Fn"
        arg: type-ref #151 11058:192:15-11074:192:31 name="HttpWire.Request"
        arg: type-ref #152 11076:192:33-11087:192:44 name="@2::RequestBody"
        arg: type-ref #154 11089:192:46-11103:192:60 name="Result"
          arg: type-ref #153 11096:192:53-11102:192:59 name="@2::Answer"
    declaration: function-decl #186 12834:210:1-13120:217:1 name="@2::client" doc="Creates a client that keeps connections open and reuses them. `options` is optional and defaults to\n`HTTP.ClientOptions()`: a 30 s `timeout_ms` for the whole call, a 10 s `connect_timeout_ms` and a 300 s\n`read_timeout_ms` for each wait for body bytes (0 for none), a 30 s idle timeout, up to 4 connections, 1 MiB bodies,\nthe system CA certificates and no proxy\n(set `tls: some(context)` to use a client context of `TLS.client_context` instead of the system certificates).\nEvery number in `options` must be positive, except `read_timeout_ms`, which is 0 (no limit) or positive;\n`max_connections` is at most 65536 and `max_body_bytes` at most 64 MiB, or the result is an error. `timeout_ms` is\nthe whole call (the wait for a slot, the connect, the head and the body); `connect_timeout_ms` bounds the connect\n(DNS, TCP, the TLS handshake and a proxy `CONNECT`) inside it, and `read_timeout_ms` each wait for body\nbytes. With a `proxy` (`http://host:port`, an origin with no path) every `http://` request goes\nthrough that forward proxy in absolute form (`GET http://host/path HTTP/1.1`, `Host` still the target's), the\nconnections are pooled by the proxy. An `https://` request opens a `CONNECT host:port` tunnel through the proxy,\none per target, and speaks TLS inside it, verified against the target (a non-2xx answer to the `CONNECT` is an\nerror that names the proxy). A `proxy` that is not an `http://` origin is an error here. The user info of the\n`proxy` (`http://user:password@host:8080`, percent-decoded) is sent to the proxy as `Proxy-Authorization: Basic`,\nand a `407` from it is an error that names the proxy and not the credentials. Close the client with `close_client`."
      param: param #160 12845:210:12-12903:210:70 name="options"
        type: type-ref #158 12854:210:21-12876:210:43 name="HttpWire.ClientOptions"
        default: construct #159 12879:210:46-12903:210:70 type="HttpWire.ClientOptions"
      result: type-ref #162 12908:210:75-12931:210:98 name="Result"
        arg: type-ref #161 12915:210:82-12930:210:97 name="HttpWire.Client"
